---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../styles/content-pages.css';

const levels = Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, index) => index + 1);
---

<Layout title="Visual Bench Methodology | Ultimate Bench" description="Detailed protocols for Visual Bench Levels 1–3: Mole, Rhino, and Chimpanzee character-localization evaluations.">
    <main class="content-page visual-methodology">
        <header class="page-heading">
            <span class="page-kicker">Benchmark protocols</span>
            <h1>Visual Bench Methodology</h1>
            <p>Visual Bench contains six progressively structured levels. This page publishes the complete protocols for Levels 1–3; documentation for Levels 4–6 is in progress.</p>
        </header>

        <nav class="level-navigation" aria-label="Visual Bench levels">
            <a href="#level-1"><span>Level 1</span><strong>Mole</strong></a>
            <a href="#level-2-rhino"><span>Level 2</span><strong>Rhino</strong></a>
            <a href="#level-3-chimpanzee"><span>Level 3</span><strong>Chimpanzee</strong></a>
            {levels.slice(3).map((level) => (
                <span class="pending"><strong>Level {level}</strong><small>Protocol documentation in progress</small></span>
            ))}
        </nav>

        <aside class="level-comparison" aria-labelledby="documented-levels-title">
            <h2 id="documented-levels-title">Documented-level comparison</h2>
            <dl>
                <div><dt>Level 1 — Mole</dt><dd>Intact 10 × 10 grid; ten fixed coordinate queries; ten identical administrations.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Level 2 — Rhino</dt><dd>Same source grid and queries; the same lateral half removed from every column; surviving strips concatenated; ten identical administrations.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Level 3 — Chimpanzee</dt><dd>Complete Level 2 image used as input; imperfect automatic background removal applied to the complete image; further nonuniform information loss and segmentation artifacts; the same coordinate queries and ten identical administrations.</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <p>Each documented level preserves the same required model behavior while changing the visual condition: Mole uses the intact grid, Rhino uses that grid after systematic removal of one lateral half of every column, and Chimpanzee processes the complete Rhino image with an imperfect automatic background-removal system.</p>
        </aside>

        <article id="level-1" class="content-card protocol" aria-labelledby="level-1-title">
            <header class="protocol-heading">
                <p class="protocol-level">Visual Bench — Level 1</p>
                <h2 id="level-1-title">Mole</h2>
                <p class="protocol-subtitle">Repeated Coordinate-Based Character Localization</p>
            </header>

            <section aria-labelledby="purpose"><h3 id="purpose">Purpose</h3>
                <p>Level 1 evaluates whether a multimodal model can consistently identify and retrieve visual entities from fixed positions within a structured two-dimensional grid.</p>
                <p>The task is designed to assess three related capabilities:</p>
                <ul><li>recognition of depicted fictional characters;</li><li>grounding of numerical coordinates within a visual grid;</li><li>consistency of responses across repeated presentations of an identical prompt.</li></ul>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="stimulus"><h3 id="stimulus">Visual stimulus</h3>
                <p>The model is presented with a fixed 10 × 10 grid containing 100 character images. All depicted characters originate from the same television series or fictional property.</p><p>The grid remains unchanged throughout the Level 1 evaluation.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="coordinates"><h3 id="coordinates">Coordinate system</h3>
                <p>Positions are defined using a one-based row–column coordinate system.</p>
                <ul><li>The first coordinate identifies the row, counted from top to bottom.</li><li>The second coordinate identifies the column, counted from left to right.</li><li>The upper-left cell is position (1,1).</li><li>The lower-right cell is position (10,10).</li></ul>
                <p>For example, position (3,7) refers to the cell in the third row and seventh column.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="prompt"><h3 id="prompt">Prompt structure</h3>
                <p>Each prompt contains the grid and ten coordinate-based questions presented simultaneously.</p><p>Each question follows the general format:</p>
                <blockquote>Which character is located at position (a,b)?</blockquote>
                <p>The model is expected to provide one identifiable character name for each of the ten requested positions.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="administration"><h3 id="administration">Repeated-administration procedure</h3>
                <p>The exact same evaluation prompt is administered ten times.</p><p>Across all ten administrations, the following elements remain unchanged:</p>
                <ul><li>the grid image;</li><li>the placement of all characters;</li><li>the ten queried coordinates;</li><li>the wording and order of the questions;</li><li>the scoring criteria.</li></ul>
                <p>The repeated use of an identical stimulus is intentional. It allows the benchmark to observe whether the model produces stable and correct answers when faced with the same visual information and instructions on multiple occasions.</p>
                <p>The evaluation therefore contains:</p><ul><li>10 unique queried positions;</li><li>10 repeated administrations of each query;</li><li>100 scored response instances in total.</li></ul>
                <p><strong>These response instances should not be interpreted as 100 independent test items.</strong> They represent ten repetitions of the same ten items.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="scoring"><h3 id="scoring">Scoring</h3>
                <p>Each response instance is scored dichotomously:</p><ul><li>1 point is awarded when the model correctly identifies the character located at the requested coordinate.</li><li>0 points are awarded when the model provides an incorrect character, omits the answer, or produces an answer that cannot be reliably matched to the requested coordinate.</li></ul>
                <p>Each administration produces a score between 0 and 10.</p><p>The final Level 1 score is the sum of all correct responses across the ten administrations:</p>
                <figure class="score-formula">
                    <math display="block" aria-label="S sub Mole equals the sum from r equals 1 to 10 of the sum from q equals 1 to 10 of x sub r q">
                        <msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>Mole</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></munderover><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></munderover><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub>
                    </math>
                    <figcaption>Final Level 1 score: correct response instances summed over ten questions in each of ten administrations.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p>where:</p><ul><li><var>r</var> represents one of the ten repeated administrations;</li><li><var>q</var> represents one of the ten coordinate questions;</li><li><var>x<sub>rq</sub></var> equals 1 for a correct response;</li><li><var>x<sub>rq</sub></var> equals 0 for an incorrect, missing, or non-evaluable response.</li></ul>
                <p>The final score ranges from 0 to 100 and is displayed as a percentage.</p><p>A score of 100 indicates that the model correctly identified all ten requested characters in all ten administrations.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="invalid"><h3 id="invalid">Invalid-result rule</h3>
                <p>A result is classified as <strong>INVALID</strong> when the model fails to produce a properly evaluable response on three consecutive administrations.</p><p>An administration may be considered non-evaluable when the model:</p>
                <ul><li>fails to answer the requested questions;</li><li>produces output that cannot be reliably associated with the ten coordinates;</li><li>returns unrelated content;</li><li>refuses the task;</li><li>experiences a severe generation or formatting failure that prevents scoring.</li></ul>
                <p>Incorrect but properly structured answers remain evaluable and receive zero points. They do not count toward the three-consecutive-failure rule.</p>
                <p>Response validity is determined through manual review. When three consecutive administrations are classified as non-evaluable, testing is terminated and the Level 1 result is recorded as INVALID.</p>
                <p>For leaderboard aggregation, INVALID is assigned a numerical value of zero. However, INVALID remains conceptually distinct from an ordinary score of zero:</p>
                <dl class="validity-comparison"><div><dt>0 points</dt><dd>The model completed the evaluation but did not answer any item correctly.</dd></div><div><dt>INVALID</dt><dd>Repeated output failures prevented the evaluation from being completed reliably.</dd></div></dl>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="interpretation"><h3 id="interpretation">Interpretation</h3>
                <p>A higher Level 1 score indicates that the model more reliably combines character recognition with absolute coordinate grounding under repeated identical conditions.</p><p>Because the same items are administered repeatedly, the final score jointly reflects:</p>
                <ul><li>answer correctness;</li><li>visual recognition stability;</li><li>coordinate-grounding stability;</li><li>response-format reliability;</li><li>susceptibility to stochastic variation between generations.</li></ul>
                <p>The score does not independently isolate accuracy from consistency. For example, a model that gives the same incorrect answer in every administration is highly consistent but receives no points, while a model that alternates between correct and incorrect answers receives partial credit.</p>
                <p>Accordingly, Level 1 should be interpreted as a measure of <strong>repeatable correct performance</strong>, rather than as a pure measure of either recognition accuracy or response consistency alone.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="limitations"><h3 id="limitations">Limitations and potential confounders</h3>
                <p>Performance may be influenced by factors other than coordinate grounding, including:</p><ul><li>the model’s prior familiarity with the depicted characters;</li><li>visual similarity between characters;</li><li>character aliases, translated names, titles, or spelling variants;</li><li>image resolution, compression, cropping, and cell dimensions;</li><li>row–column transposition errors;</li><li>loss of answer order in the multi-question response;</li><li>subjective decisions during manual review;</li><li>stochastic changes in model output across repeated generations.</li></ul>
                <p>The fixed grid and fixed coordinates improve comparability between administrations and models. However, they also limit the breadth of the visual sample. Results therefore describe performance on this specific Level 1 stimulus and should not be treated as a comprehensive measure of visual-spatial ability.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="record"><h3 id="record">Recommended result record</h3>
                <p>For each model, the Level 1 evaluation record should preserve:</p><ul><li>benchmark and stimulus version;</li><li>exact model name and version;</li><li>date of testing;</li><li>model configuration and reasoning settings;</li><li>availability of tools or code execution;</li><li>score obtained in each of the ten administrations;</li><li>total score;</li><li>original model responses;</li><li>accepted character-name variants;</li><li>non-evaluable administrations and their reasons;</li><li>whether the final result was classified as INVALID.</li></ul>
            </section>
        </article>

        <article id="level-2-rhino" class="content-card protocol" aria-labelledby="level-2-rhino-title">
            <header class="protocol-heading">
                <p class="protocol-level">Visual Bench — Level 2: Rhino</p>
                <h2 id="level-2-rhino-title">Rhino</h2>
                <p class="protocol-subtitle">Repeated Coordinate-Based Character Localization Under Systematic Lateral Cropping</p>
            </header>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-purpose"><h3 id="rhino-purpose">Purpose</h3>
                <p>Level 2 evaluates whether a multimodal model can preserve correct and repeatable character localization when a substantial and systematically defined portion of the visual input has been removed.</p>
                <p>The task retains the coordinate-query structure of Level 1 while introducing a controlled visual degradation. It primarily assesses:</p>
                <ul><li>recognition of characters from partial visual evidence;</li><li>robustness to systematic loss of image information;</li><li>absolute coordinate grounding within an altered grid geometry;</li><li>consistency across repeated presentations of an identical degraded stimulus.</li></ul>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-relationship"><h3 id="rhino-relationship">Relationship to Level 1</h3>
                <p>Level 2 uses the same underlying character grid, coordinate system, prompt structure, repeated-administration procedure, scoring method, and invalid-result rule as Level 1.</p>
                <p>The defining difference is that the original square grid is transformed before being presented to the model.</p>
                <p>This makes Level 2 a controlled degradation condition relative to the intact Level 1 stimulus.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-transformation"><h3 id="rhino-transformation">Visual transformation</h3>
                <p>The source stimulus is a 10 × 10 grid containing 100 character images.</p>
                <p>Each of the ten vertical grid columns is divided longitudinally into two equal-width halves. The same lateral half is retained for every column, while the other half is removed.</p>
                <p>The ten surviving vertical strips are then concatenated without restoring the deleted regions.</p>
                <p>As a result:</p>
                <ul><li>the logical 10-row × 10-column organization is preserved;</li><li>the original ordering of cells is preserved;</li><li>each cell contains approximately 50% of its original horizontal visual information;</li><li>the transformed grid retains its original height but has approximately half of its original width;</li><li>the final stimulus is rectangular rather than square.</li></ul>
                <p>The transformation therefore reduces the quantity of available visual information while preserving the coordinate identities and relative ordering of all 100 cells.</p>
                <p>The benchmark record should specify whether the retained region is the left or right lateral half of each original column.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-coordinates"><h3 id="rhino-coordinates">Coordinate system</h3>
                <p>The transformed stimulus continues to use a one-based row–column coordinate system.</p>
                <p>The first coordinate identifies the row, counted from top to bottom.<br />The second coordinate identifies the logical column, counted from left to right.<br />The upper-left cell is position (1,1).<br />The lower-right cell is position (10,10).</p>
                <p>Although the visible cells are narrower than in Level 1, the logical grid remains 10 × 10. Coordinates therefore refer to the preserved logical cell structure rather than to equal square regions of the final image.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-prompt"><h3 id="rhino-prompt">Prompt structure</h3>
                <p>Each administration contains the transformed grid and the same ten coordinate-based questions used in Level 1.</p>
                <p>Each question follows the general format:</p>
                <blockquote>Which character is located at position (a,b)?</blockquote>
                <p>The model is expected to provide one identifiable character name for each requested position.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-administration"><h3 id="rhino-administration">Repeated-administration procedure</h3>
                <p>The exact same transformed grid and the exact same set of ten questions are administered ten times.</p>
                <p>Across all ten administrations, the following remain unchanged:</p>
                <ul><li>the transformed grid image;</li><li>the retained lateral half of each column;</li><li>the placement and ordering of the character fragments;</li><li>the ten queried coordinates;</li><li>the wording and order of the questions;</li><li>the scoring criteria.</li></ul>
                <p>The repetition is intentional. It measures whether the model can repeatedly produce correct answers from the same incomplete visual evidence.</p>
                <p>The evaluation contains:</p>
                <ul><li>10 unique queried positions;</li><li>10 repeated administrations of each query;</li><li>100 scored response instances.</li></ul>
                <p>These 100 response instances are repeated measurements of ten unique items and should not be interpreted as 100 independent visual questions.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-scoring"><h3 id="rhino-scoring">Scoring</h3>
                <p>Each response instance is scored dichotomously:</p>
                <p>1 point is awarded when the model correctly identifies the character located at the requested coordinate.<br />0 points are awarded when the model provides an incorrect character, omits the answer, or produces an answer that cannot be reliably associated with the requested coordinate.</p>
                <p>Each administration produces a score between 0 and 10.</p>
                <p>The final Level 2 score is the total number of correct responses across all ten administrations:</p>
                <figure class="score-formula">
                    <math display="block" aria-label="S sub Rhino equals the sum from r equals 1 to 10 of the sum from q equals 1 to 10 of x sub r q">
                        <msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>Rhino</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></munderover><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></munderover><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub>
                    </math>
                    <figcaption>Final Level 2 score: correct response instances summed over ten questions in each of ten administrations.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p>where:</p>
                <ul><li><var>r</var> represents one of the ten repeated administrations;</li><li><var>q</var> represents one of the ten coordinate questions;</li><li><var>x<sub>rq</sub> = 1</var> for a correct response;</li><li><var>x<sub>rq</sub> = 0</var> for an incorrect, missing, or non-evaluable response.</li></ul>
                <p>The final score ranges from 0 to 100 and is displayed as a percentage.</p>
                <p>A score of 100 indicates that the model correctly identified all ten partial character views in all ten administrations.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-invalid"><h3 id="rhino-invalid">Invalid-result rule</h3>
                <p>A result is classified as <strong>INVALID</strong> when the model fails to produce a properly evaluable response on three consecutive administrations.</p>
                <p>An administration may be considered non-evaluable when the model:</p>
                <ul><li>fails to answer the requested questions;</li><li>produces output that cannot be reliably associated with the ten coordinates;</li><li>returns unrelated content;</li><li>refuses the task;</li><li>experiences a severe generation or formatting failure that prevents scoring.</li></ul>
                <p>Incorrect but properly structured answers remain evaluable and receive zero points. They do not count toward the three-consecutive-failure rule.</p>
                <p>Response validity is determined through manual review. When three consecutive administrations are classified as non-evaluable, testing is terminated and the Level 2 result is recorded as INVALID.</p>
                <p>For leaderboard aggregation, INVALID is assigned a numerical value of zero while remaining conceptually distinct from a completed score of zero.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-interpretation"><h3 id="rhino-interpretation">Interpretation</h3>
                <p>A higher Level 2 score indicates that the model more reliably combines partial-view character recognition with absolute coordinate grounding under repeated identical conditions.</p>
                <p>The score reflects a combination of:</p>
                <ul><li>the ability to recognize characters from incomplete lateral views;</li><li>robustness to the removal of approximately half of the horizontal visual evidence;</li><li>adaptation to narrower cell geometry and a changed overall aspect ratio;</li><li>preservation of row–column coordinate interpretation;</li><li>response stability across repeated generations.</li></ul>
                <p>Level 2 should be interpreted as a measure of repeatable correct performance under systematic visual information loss.</p>
                <p>It should not be interpreted as a pure measure of character recognition, spatial reasoning, perceptual completion, or general robustness to real-world occlusion.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-comparison"><h3 id="rhino-comparison">Comparison with Level 1</h3>
                <p>Because Levels 1 and 2 preserve the same underlying grid, coordinates, questions, repetition count, and scoring method, their difference provides a controlled estimate of performance degradation caused by the Level 2 transformation.</p>
                <p>Two supplementary comparison measures may be reported without replacing the official Level 2 score.</p>
                <h4>Absolute performance loss</h4>
                <figure class="score-formula">
                    <math display="block" aria-label="D from Level 1 to Level 2 equals the Level 1 score minus the Level 2 score"><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L1</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L2</mtext></msub></math>
                    <figcaption>Absolute performance loss from Level 1 to Level 2.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p>This indicates how many percentage points the model loses after systematic lateral cropping.</p>
                <h4>Performance retention</h4>
                <figure class="score-formula">
                    <math display="block" aria-label="R from Level 1 to Level 2 equals the Level 2 score divided by the Level 1 score, multiplied by 100"><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L2</mtext></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L1</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn></math>
                    <figcaption>Performance retained under the Level 2 degradation condition.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p>This indicates the proportion of the model’s Level 1 performance retained under the Level 2 degradation condition.</p>
                <p>Performance retention should be reported only when the Level 1 score is greater than zero. It is a supplementary robustness measure and not a replacement for the official Level 2 score.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-limitations"><h3 id="rhino-limitations">Limitations and potential confounders</h3>
                <p>Performance may be influenced by:</p>
                <ul><li>whether the retained half contains the most distinctive features of particular characters;</li><li>asymmetric hairstyles, facial markings, clothing, accessories, or poses;</li><li>the choice between retaining the left or right side;</li><li>variation in how centrally characters are positioned inside their cells;</li><li>the model’s prior familiarity with the depicted characters;</li><li>narrower character fragments being visually merged with neighboring strips;</li><li>the changed aspect ratio of the complete grid;</li><li>row–column transposition or answer-order errors;</li><li>aliases, translated names, titles, abbreviations, or spelling variants;</li><li>subjective decisions during manual scoring;</li><li>stochastic variation across repeated generations.</li></ul>
                <p>Because the same lateral half is removed from every column, the manipulation is systematic and reproducible. However, it may advantage characters whose most diagnostic features happen to appear on the retained side.</p>
                <p>The test also cannot determine whether a correct response was produced through genuine completion of the missing visual structure or through recognition of one remaining distinctive feature.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="rhino-record"><h3 id="rhino-record">Recommended result record</h3>
                <p>For each model, the Level 2 evaluation record should preserve:</p>
                <ul><li>benchmark and stimulus version;</li><li>identity of the source Level 1 grid;</li><li>exact image-transformation procedure;</li><li>whether the left or right half was retained;</li><li>transformed image dimensions;</li><li>exact model name and version;</li><li>date of testing;</li><li>model configuration and reasoning settings;</li><li>availability of tools or code execution;</li><li>score obtained in each administration;</li><li>total Level 2 score;</li><li>corresponding Level 1 score;</li><li>absolute performance loss;</li><li>performance retention, where calculable;</li><li>original model responses;</li><li>accepted character-name variants;</li><li>non-evaluable administrations and their reasons;</li><li>whether the final result was classified as INVALID.</li></ul>
            </section>
        </article>
        <article id="level-3-chimpanzee" class="content-card protocol" aria-labelledby="level-3-chimpanzee-title">
            <header class="protocol-heading">
                <p class="protocol-level">Visual Bench — Level 3: Chimpanzee</p>
                <h2 id="level-3-chimpanzee-title">Chimpanzee</h2>
                <p class="protocol-subtitle">Repeated Coordinate-Based Character Localization Under Compound Cropping and Automated Segmentation Degradation</p>
            </header>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-purpose"><h3 id="chimp-purpose">Purpose</h3>
                <p>Level 3 evaluates whether a multimodal model can preserve correct and repeatable character localization after the visual stimulus has undergone two successive forms of information degradation:</p>
                <ul><li>systematic lateral cropping, as defined in Level 2; and</li><li>imperfect automatic background removal applied to the complete transformed image.</li></ul>
                <p>The task retains the coordinate-query structure used in Levels 1 and 2 while further reducing and distorting the visual evidence available to the model.</p>
                <p>Level 3 primarily assesses:</p>
                <ul><li>recognition of characters from incomplete and artifact-degraded visual evidence;</li><li>robustness to additional information loss produced by imperfect foreground segmentation;</li><li>preservation of absolute coordinate grounding after compound image transformation;</li><li>consistency across repeated presentations of an identical degraded stimulus.</li></ul>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-relationship"><h3 id="chimp-relationship">Relationship to Levels 1 and 2</h3>
                <p>Level 3 uses the same underlying character arrangement, coordinate system, ten queried positions, question wording, question order, repetition count, scoring method, and invalid-result rule as Levels 1 and 2.</p>
                <p>The stimulus is derived directly from the completed Level 2 image.</p>
                <p>The Level 2 transformation first removes the same lateral half of every original grid column and concatenates the ten surviving vertical strips.</p>
                <p>An automatic background-removal tool is then applied once to the complete Level 2 composite image.</p>
                <p>Level 3 is therefore a compound degradation condition:</p>
                <ul><li>Level 1 presents the intact grid;</li><li>Level 2 introduces systematic lateral information loss;</li><li>Level 3 retains the Level 2 cropping and adds imperfect automated segmentation.</li></ul>
                <p>This design permits direct comparison of model performance across progressively degraded versions of the same underlying visual task.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-source"><h3 id="chimp-source">Source stimulus</h3>
                <p>The source for Level 3 is the final transformed image used in Level 2.</p>
                <p>That Level 2 stimulus retains the logical organization of:</p>
                <ul><li>10 rows;</li><li>10 columns;</li><li>100 character positions.</li></ul>
                <p>Although the image is narrower than the original Level 1 grid, the character ordering and coordinate identities remain unchanged.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-transformation"><h3 id="chimp-transformation">Automated background-removal transformation</h3>
                <p>The complete Level 2 image is processed by an automatic background-removal tool.</p>
                <p>The tool is intentionally imperfect. Its output may remove not only background information but also portions of the depicted characters or other visually informative image regions.</p>
                <p>Depending on the segmentation errors produced by the tool, the transformed image may contain:</p>
                <ul><li>missing foreground pixels;</li><li>erosion of character boundaries;</li><li>incomplete faces, clothing, hair, or accessories;</li><li>fragmented silhouettes;</li><li>irregular transparent or blank regions;</li><li>loss of color or texture information;</li><li>residual background fragments;</li><li>edge halos or other segmentation artifacts;</li><li>unintended merging or separation of nearby visual regions.</li></ul>
                <p>The exact artifact pattern is determined by the output of the selected automatic background-removal process.</p>
                <p>The same completed Level 3 image is used for every model and every repeated administration within the same benchmark version.</p>
                <p>No attempt should be made to manually correct the segmentation output after generation unless such corrections are explicitly defined as part of a new stimulus version.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-compound"><h3 id="chimp-compound">Compound nature of the degradation</h3>
                <p>Level 3 does not isolate the effect of background removal on an otherwise intact image.</p>
                <p>The automatic segmentation process is applied after the Level 2 lateral-cropping transformation. Consequently, Level 3 performance reflects the combined effects of:</p>
                <ul><li>information already removed through systematic lateral cropping;</li><li>additional foreground and contextual information removed by the segmentation system;</li><li>visual artifacts introduced by the segmentation process;</li><li>the altered rectangular geometry inherited from Level 2.</li></ul>
                <p>Level 3 should therefore be described as a compound degradation condition rather than as a pure test of background-removal robustness.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-structure"><h3 id="chimp-structure">Preservation of logical structure</h3>
                <p>Despite the additional image degradation, the logical 10 × 10 organization remains unchanged.</p>
                <p>The first coordinate identifies the row, counted from top to bottom.<br />The second coordinate identifies the logical column, counted from left to right.<br />The upper-left position remains (1,1).<br />The lower-right position remains (10,10).</p>
                <p>Coordinates refer to the preserved logical position of each character, even when the visible character fragment has been substantially damaged by the transformation.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-prompt"><h3 id="chimp-prompt">Prompt structure</h3>
                <p>Each administration contains the Level 3 image and the same ten coordinate-based questions used in Levels 1 and 2.</p>
                <p>Each question follows the general format:</p>
                <blockquote>Which character is located at position (a,b)?</blockquote>
                <p>The model is expected to provide one identifiable character name for each of the ten requested positions.</p>
                <p>The required model behavior does not change between Levels 1, 2, and 3. Only the visual stimulus is transformed.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-administration"><h3 id="chimp-administration">Repeated-administration procedure</h3>
                <p>The exact same Level 3 image and the exact same ten questions are administered ten times.</p>
                <p>Across all ten administrations, the following remain unchanged:</p>
                <ul><li>the degraded image;</li><li>the segmentation artifacts;</li><li>the placement and ordering of the surviving visual fragments;</li><li>the ten queried coordinates;</li><li>the wording and order of the questions;</li><li>the scoring criteria.</li></ul>
                <p>The repetition is intentional. It measures whether the model can repeatedly produce correct answers from the same heavily degraded visual evidence.</p>
                <p>The evaluation contains:</p>
                <ul><li>10 unique queried positions;</li><li>10 repeated administrations of each query;</li><li>100 scored response instances.</li></ul>
                <p>These 100 response instances are repeated measurements of ten unique items. They must not be described as 100 independent visual questions.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-scoring"><h3 id="chimp-scoring">Scoring</h3>
                <p>Each response instance is scored dichotomously:</p>
                <ul><li>1 point is awarded when the model correctly identifies the character located at the requested coordinate.</li><li>0 points are awarded when the model provides an incorrect character, omits the answer, or produces an answer that cannot be reliably associated with the requested coordinate.</li></ul>
                <p>Each administration produces a score between 0 and 10.</p>
                <p>The final Level 3 score is the total number of correct responses across all ten administrations:</p>
                <figure class="score-formula">
                    <math display="block" aria-label="S sub Chimpanzee equals the sum from r equals 1 to 10 of the sum from q equals 1 to 10 of x sub r q"><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>Chimpanzee</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></munderover><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></munderover><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></math>
                    <figcaption>Final Level 3 score: correct response instances summed over ten questions in each of ten administrations.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p>where:</p>
                <ul><li><var>r</var> represents one of the ten repeated administrations;</li><li><var>q</var> represents one of the ten coordinate questions;</li><li><var>x<sub>rq</sub> = 1</var> for a correct response;</li><li><var>x<sub>rq</sub> = 0</var> for an incorrect, missing, or non-evaluable response.</li></ul>
                <p>The final score ranges from 0 to 100 and is displayed as a percentage.</p>
                <p>A score of 100 indicates that the model correctly identified all ten requested characters in all ten administrations despite the compound visual degradation.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-invalid"><h3 id="chimp-invalid">Invalid-result rule</h3>
                <p>A result is classified as <strong>INVALID</strong> when the model fails to produce a properly evaluable response on three consecutive administrations.</p>
                <p>An administration may be considered non-evaluable when the model:</p>
                <ul><li>fails to answer the requested questions;</li><li>produces output that cannot be reliably associated with the ten coordinates;</li><li>returns unrelated content;</li><li>refuses the task;</li><li>experiences a severe generation or formatting failure that prevents scoring.</li></ul>
                <p>Incorrect but properly structured answers remain evaluable and receive zero points. They do not count toward the three-consecutive-failure rule.</p>
                <p>Response validity is determined through manual review.</p>
                <p>When three consecutive administrations are classified as non-evaluable, testing is terminated and the Level 3 result is recorded as INVALID.</p>
                <p>For leaderboard aggregation, INVALID is assigned a numerical value of zero while remaining conceptually distinct from a completed score of zero:</p>
                <dl class="validity-comparison"><div><dt>0 points</dt><dd>The model completed the evaluation but produced no correct answers.</dd></div><div><dt>INVALID</dt><dd>Repeated response failures prevented reliable completion of the evaluation.</dd></div></dl>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-interpretation"><h3 id="chimp-interpretation">Interpretation</h3>
                <p>A higher Level 3 score indicates that the model more reliably combines character recognition and absolute coordinate grounding when the available visual evidence has been degraded by both systematic cropping and imperfect automated segmentation.</p>
                <p>The score reflects a combination of:</p>
                <ul><li>recognition from incomplete character fragments;</li><li>robustness to foreground-information loss;</li><li>robustness to segmentation artifacts;</li><li>adaptation to the altered geometry inherited from Level 2;</li><li>preservation of row–column coordinate interpretation;</li><li>response stability across repeated generations.</li></ul>
                <p>Level 3 should be interpreted as a measure of: <strong>repeatable correct character localization under compound visual information loss and segmentation artifacts.</strong></p>
                <p>It should not be interpreted as a pure measure of:</p>
                <ul><li>background-removal robustness;</li><li>object completion;</li><li>character recognition alone;</li><li>spatial reasoning alone;</li><li>general resistance to all forms of image corruption;</li><li>performance under natural real-world occlusion.</li></ul>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-difficulty"><h3 id="chimp-difficulty">Why the task is harder than Level 2</h3>
                <p>Level 2 removes a predetermined and geometrically regular portion of the image.</p>
                <p>Level 3 introduces an additional transformation whose information loss may be spatially irregular and semantically uneven.</p>
                <p>The automatic background-removal system may preserve some character features while incorrectly removing others. Consequently, different characters may retain different amounts and types of identifying information.</p>
                <p>This can make Level 3 more difficult because the model must interpret:</p>
                <ul><li>already cropped character fragments;</li><li>incomplete or damaged foreground features;</li><li>irregular boundaries;</li><li>missing contextual information;</li><li>potentially misleading segmentation remnants.</li></ul>
                <p>However, the protocol should not claim that every queried character is necessarily more degraded than in Level 2. The effect of automatic segmentation may differ between cells and characters.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-level-2-comparison"><h3 id="chimp-level-2-comparison">Comparison with Level 2</h3>
                <p>Because Levels 2 and 3 use the same underlying transformed grid, logical coordinates, questions, repetition count, and scoring method, the score difference provides a controlled estimate of the additional performance loss associated with the background-removal transformation.</p>
                <h4>Additional absolute performance loss</h4>
                <figure class="score-formula"><math display="block" aria-label="Level 2 score minus Level 3 score"><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L2</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L3</mtext></msub></math><figcaption>Additional absolute performance loss from Level 2 to Level 3.</figcaption></figure>
                <p>This indicates the number of percentage points lost between Level 2 and Level 3.</p>
                <h4>Level 3 retention relative to Level 2</h4>
                <figure class="score-formula"><math display="block" aria-label="Level 3 score divided by Level 2 score, multiplied by 100"><mfrac><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L3</mtext></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L2</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn></math><figcaption>Proportion of Level 2 performance retained after the additional segmentation degradation.</figcaption></figure>
                <p>This indicates the proportion of Level 2 performance retained after the additional segmentation degradation.</p>
                <p>Retention should be reported only when the Level 2 score is greater than zero and is not INVALID.</p>
                <p>These are supplementary analyses. They do not replace the official Level 3 score and must not alter the current leaderboard-ranking logic.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-level-1-comparison"><h3 id="chimp-level-1-comparison">Comparison with Level 1</h3>
                <p>An additional descriptive comparison may be made between the intact Level 1 stimulus and the compound Level 3 condition.</p>
                <h4>Total absolute performance loss</h4>
                <figure class="score-formula"><math display="block" aria-label="Level 1 score minus Level 3 score"><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L1</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>L3</mtext></msub></math><figcaption>Total absolute performance loss from Level 1 to Level 3.</figcaption></figure>
                <p>This describes the total score reduction between the intact grid and the final compound-degradation condition.</p>
                <p>Because two different transformations separate Levels 1 and 3, this difference must not be attributed exclusively to the automatic background-removal process.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-limitations"><h3 id="chimp-limitations">Limitations and potential confounders</h3>
                <p>Performance may be influenced by:</p>
                <ul><li>the specific background-removal tool used;</li><li>the version and configuration of that tool;</li><li>whether processing occurred locally or through an external service;</li><li>the image format supplied to the tool;</li><li>the resolution of the input and output images;</li><li>resizing or compression performed before or after segmentation;</li><li>transparency handling in the final image;</li><li>the color displayed behind transparent regions;</li><li>whether the retained character half contains distinctive features;</li><li>character-specific differences in segmentation quality;</li><li>similarities between surviving character fragments;</li><li>loss of contextual clues that previously supported recognition;</li><li>residual artifacts that accidentally provide useful identifying information;</li><li>the model’s prior familiarity with the depicted characters;</li><li>aliases, translated names, titles, abbreviations, or spelling variants;</li><li>row–column transposition and answer-order errors;</li><li>subjective decisions during manual scoring;</li><li>stochastic variation across repeated generations.</li></ul>
                <p>Because the background-removal tool is applied to the complete Level 2 image rather than independently to each character cell, segmentation behavior may also be influenced by neighboring regions and by the composite structure of the grid.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-scope"><h3 id="chimp-scope">Scope of the conclusions</h3>
                <p>Level 3 demonstrates how models perform on one fixed image after one particular compound-transformation procedure.</p>
                <p>It does not by itself establish that a model is generally robust to:</p>
                <ul><li>all background-removal algorithms;</li><li>all segmentation errors;</li><li>all forms of missing visual information;</li><li>natural occlusion;</li><li>arbitrary image corruption;</li><li>unfamiliar character sets.</li></ul>
                <p>The findings should be limited to the documented stimulus, transformation process, model configuration, and benchmark version.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-reproducibility"><h3 id="chimp-reproducibility">Reproducibility requirements</h3>
                <p>The Level 3 stimulus record should preserve:</p>
                <ul><li>the exact Level 1 source image;</li><li>the exact Level 2 transformed image;</li><li>the final Level 3 image;</li><li>the name of the background-removal tool;</li><li>the tool version, where available;</li><li>the exact processing date;</li><li>relevant settings or options;</li><li>whether the tool was used locally or through a web service;</li><li>input and output file formats;</li><li>input and output dimensions;</li><li>transparency and background-color handling;</li><li>cryptographic hashes for all stimulus files;</li><li>confirmation that no manual corrections were applied;</li><li>a new version identifier whenever the transformation is regenerated.</li></ul>
                <p>If the exact background-removal tool or settings were not previously recorded, do not invent them. Clearly mark those historical details as unavailable and ensure they are recorded for future benchmark versions.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="chimp-record"><h3 id="chimp-record">Recommended result record</h3>
                <p>For each model, the Level 3 evaluation record should preserve:</p>
                <ul><li>benchmark and stimulus version;</li><li>identity of the corresponding Level 1 and Level 2 stimuli;</li><li>exact model name and version;</li><li>date of testing;</li><li>model configuration and reasoning settings;</li><li>availability of tools or code execution;</li><li>score obtained in each of the ten administrations;</li><li>total Level 3 score;</li><li>corresponding Level 1 and Level 2 scores;</li><li>additional absolute loss from Level 2 to Level 3;</li><li>Level 3 retention relative to Level 2, where calculable;</li><li>total absolute loss from Level 1 to Level 3;</li><li>original model responses;</li><li>accepted character-name variants;</li><li>non-evaluable administrations and their reasons;</li><li>whether the final result was classified as INVALID.</li></ul>
            </section>
        </article>
    </main>
</Layout>

